WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.175
Ask who owns compliance and the
answer is often the security office,

2
00:00:04.175 --> 00:00:06.238
which means nobody in particular.

3
00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:10.049
The Risk Management Framework
names an owner for every task.

4
00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:14.803
The system owner owns more of those
tasks than any other role: categorizing,

5
00:00:14.803 --> 00:00:18.713
selecting and implementing
controls, remediating findings,

6
00:00:18.713 --> 00:00:22.163
keeping the plan of action,
and assembling the package.

7
00:00:22.163 --> 00:00:27.209
The CISO sets control requirements and
the common controls that systems inherit,

8
00:00:27.209 --> 00:00:30.938
and reviews providers' quarterly
reports or names a delegate.

9
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:34.272
The privacy official approves
the categorization and

10
00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:37.750
the privacy plan of any system
with personal information.

11
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:41.769
The authorizing official is the only
official who can accept the risk,

12
00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:43.987
and that acceptance can't be delegated.

13
00:00:43.987 --> 00:00:49.108
The official, or the designated representative,
also approves the categorization,

14
00:00:49.108 --> 00:00:51.186
the plans and the assessment plan,

15
00:00:51.186 --> 00:00:54.600
and selects the assessor,
before the assessment starts.

16
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.745
Six roles are government-only,

17
00:00:56.745 --> 00:01:00.863
among them the authorizing
official, the C I O and the CISO.

18
00:01:00.863 --> 00:01:04.761
A contractor-heavy team can
prepare every plan and package,

19
00:01:04.761 --> 00:01:06.750
but can't sign those decisions.

20
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.239
And keep the roles apart:
nobody assesses their own work,

21
00:01:10.239 --> 00:01:13.560
and the authorizing official
isn't the system owner.
